<h3>How much wardrobe space do you need?</h3>
Start by looking at what you actually own. Someone with a large collection of clothes will need very different storage from someone with a more minimal wardrobe.

Think about how much hanging space you need, as well as drawers for folded clothing and smaller items. If you share the bedroom, dividing the storage between two people can also help you work out how much wardrobe space is required.

A full-height wardrobe can be particularly useful because it takes advantage of vertical space without taking up additional floor area. Understanding <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/how-much-does-bespoke-joinery-cost-in-london-in-2026/">how much bespoke joinery costs in London</a> helps homeowners budget accurately for custom built-ins that run seamlessly floor to ceiling.
<h3>Should you use built-in or freestanding storage?</h3>
Built-in storage can be a practical choice when working with awkward bedroom dimensions. It can be designed around chimney breasts, sloping ceilings, alcoves or other features that would otherwise become difficult to use.

Freestanding furniture offers more flexibility, but it can sometimes leave unused gaps around the room. On projects requiring structural reconfiguration, opting for <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/design-and-build/">integrated design and build London</a> solutions allows bedroom partitions and wardrobes to be constructed harmoniously.

